WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.820
- No question,

00:00:01.820 --> 00:00:03.750
the cannabis industry is ahead of almost

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:08.750
every other industry
because of its general youth

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:11.783
and pure bred entrepreneurship.

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:15.923
You've got your perspective.

00:00:19.407 --> 00:00:20.240
I just want to be happy.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:21.370
Don't you wanna be happy?

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:26.840
I love starting with the macro

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:28.340
and then going into the micro.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:33.180
My college friends are
gonna laugh their asses off

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:34.710
that I'm on the cover of your magazine,

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:37.840
because literally their four-year quest

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:40.603
in college was to get me
to smoke a blunt with them.

00:00:42.041 --> 00:00:46.460
So this is unbelievably deja vu for me.

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:49.960
I grew up not drinking
beer, or wine, or alcohol,

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:54.084
yet, I became an
unbelievable expert at 15,

00:00:54.084 --> 00:00:55.334
17, 19, 21, 23,

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:00.273
on the consumer of that product.

00:01:02.885 --> 00:01:05.740
So it's unbelievable to me in the macro

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:10.370
that it's so obvious that the
cannabis industry is heading

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:13.080
in an incredibly
interesting consumer place

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:14.653
over the next half century.

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:18.500
And I'm not in a rush to make predictions

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:19.770
or known everything just yet,

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:22.070
'cause this is a real marathon for me.

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:23.090
I'm 42 years old,

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:27.560
and I think that some of
my most intriguing thoughts

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:28.910
or observations,

00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:31.230
or accomplishments within the industry

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:33.363
literally may happen in 30 or 40 years.

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:36.970
- [Andrea] So, Gary, are both
of your folks still alive?

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:37.803
- Yeah, they are.

00:01:37.803 --> 00:01:39.180
They're pretty young actually.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:40.450
They're 64 and 62,

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:41.800
so they're young.

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:43.341
- [Andrea] What will they think about you

00:01:43.341 --> 00:01:45.250
being on the cover of a cannabis magazine?

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:47.760
- You know my mom was
super propaganda'd out

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:49.090
by Nancy Reagan.

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:51.120
So she was super hardcore,

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:52.550
like, say no to drugs.

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:55.080
And so I think,

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:56.480
look, I think my parents know,

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:58.090
first of all,

00:01:58.090 --> 00:01:59.620
the great thing about my parents is,

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:01.010
and I just spent a week with them

00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:02.283
because of vacation.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:05.370
They're very thoughtful,

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:07.580
meaning, like, I genuinely believe

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:09.270
with my heart and soul

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:13.930
that cannabis is a far better
thing for so many human beings

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:15.530
than a lot of the things that are legal

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:16.580
in the United States,

00:02:17.935 --> 00:02:18.910
on a federal level.

00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:22.790
And so my parents are thoughtful that way.

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:26.240
My mom can get caught
up in the stigma of it.

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:29.680
I think right now if you actually
like lie detector test me,

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:30.930
here's what I would say,

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:31.967
my mom would be like,

00:02:31.967 --> 00:02:33.400
"Hmm."

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:35.607
And my dad would be like, "He's a genius.

00:02:35.607 --> 00:02:36.847
"That's gonna work out."

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:39.200
Which is really interesting,

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:42.100
'cause that would be
literally the opposite

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:43.150
of almost everything else.

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:45.320
My mom is my greatest cheerleader

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:46.670
and thinks I'm a genius.

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:47.890
But I definitely think,

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:50.123
I'm telling you Nancy Reagan got to her.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:52.740
- [Andrea] Well, Nancy
Reagan was a smart lady.

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:54.590
She had a message to
send and she did it well.

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:55.423
That's for sure.

00:02:55.423 --> 00:02:57.010
- No question.

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:59.820
- [Andrea] So, at some point before 2012,

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:02.130
you had the realization
that corporate America

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:05.213
was slow to pick up on
content strategy trends.

00:03:06.311 --> 00:03:08.853
Do you think cannabis is
making a similar mistake?

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:13.680
- No, actually I think cannabis,

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:16.140
from my observation early on,

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:17.460
is really good at it,

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:19.510
because you have such young entrepreneurs

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:21.930
who are native to that communication.

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:25.870
I think the platforms;
Facebook, Google, and such,

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:28.990
don't let them advertise in all the ways

00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:30.390
they wish they could.

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:32.640
But actually I think the cannabis industry

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:36.970
is going to be the teacher
of innovation to a lot of,

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:38.290
I view the cannabis industry

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:41.130
the way I view Procter
and Gamble and Coca Cola,

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:42.880
consumer package goods.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:44.600
And I think that from,

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:46.170
you know, obviously
depending on what happens

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:47.210
at a federal state level,

00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:51.820
but I think they're going
to innovate and show.

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:54.870
I mean, I'm very comfortable
making this prediction.

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:58.970
In 2040, cannabis companies
are gonna be the ones

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:00.900
influencing the way diapers,

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:04.983
and tires, and soda, and
shampoo is being sold.

00:04:04.983 --> 00:04:07.999
- [Andrea] So you often say
that people aren't marketing

00:04:07.999 --> 00:04:09.499
in the year that they live in.

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:12.190
Any advice for cannabis companies

00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:15.913
wanting to market their
brands in 2018 and beyond?

00:04:17.830 --> 00:04:20.510
- Well, you know, again,

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:24.040
they can't run the paid
advertising on Facebook and YouTube

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:25.370
that I want them to.

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:29.000
So it's about influencer
marketing and organic creative

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:30.590
within the Facebook,

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:33.080
since we have Snapchats of the world.

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:34.372
The end.

00:04:34.372 --> 00:04:35.205
And I'll say this again.

00:04:35.205 --> 00:04:36.800
No question,

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:38.730
the cannabis industry is ahead of almost

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:40.870
every other industry,

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:45.870
because of its general youth
and pure bred entrepreneurship.

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:53.840
- [Andrea] You said that one thing

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:56.820
that scares the fuck out
of you are nice watches

00:04:56.820 --> 00:04:57.653
and Ferraris.

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:01.250
What is one piece of advice
you would give to someone

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:03.543
who is motivated by material possessions?

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:08.393
- You know, to really create
context around that statement,

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:12.230
anybody who buys a nice
watch, or a Ferrari,

00:05:12.230 --> 00:05:16.790
or a big house because
they're disguising insecurity,

00:05:16.790 --> 00:05:18.370
is my great fear.

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:21.430
Using material things to put a bandaid

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:24.263
on your emotional
shortcomings is very scary.

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:28.640
And so my piece of advice is,

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:33.230
figure out that buying
a $900 Supreme backpack

00:05:33.230 --> 00:05:36.093
isn't gonna trick the smartest people.

00:05:39.670 --> 00:05:40.503
- [Andrea] And I was watching,

00:05:40.503 --> 00:05:45.503
I think it was your South
by Southwest talk last year,

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:47.290
or might've even been in 2016.

00:05:49.670 --> 00:05:54.670
And you often talk about the
marathon versus the sprint,

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:59.550
and how building a plan
is more advantageous

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:01.773
than spreading your,

00:06:02.710 --> 00:06:04.440
and focusing on one location,

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:07.983
but trying to, essentially,
grow too big for your britches.

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:12.407
I guess I'm just curious on,

00:06:12.407 --> 00:06:13.960
I'd like to hear more,

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:16.070
like the importance of branding

00:06:16.070 --> 00:06:17.620
and what advice you'd give,

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:19.926
and how people can do it well.

00:06:19.926 --> 00:06:21.370
- You know, I think you and I share this.

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:23.970
And I'd be lying if I didn't tell you

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:26.020
that I'm aware of a lot of the people

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:27.710
that are in the industry,

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:31.970
and that my voice is uniquely
beneficial to this industry,

00:06:31.970 --> 00:06:34.120
because you have an
enormous amount of people

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:35.450
in the industry right now

00:06:35.450 --> 00:06:37.823
that are so obnoxiously short term,

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:42.960
that you want your
forefathers to be good, right?

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:44.850
Because then you have healthier industry.

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:46.510
And, so, it's funny.

00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:48.050
I look at emerging markets,

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:51.430
whether it's the emerging gambling market

00:06:51.430 --> 00:06:53.770
that we're gonna see because
of the Supreme Court ruling,

00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:56.240
cryptocurrency, cannabis.

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:57.860
What happens in these markets,

00:06:57.860 --> 00:07:02.330
is often times you get 3% to 4% pioneers

00:07:02.330 --> 00:07:04.740
and 96% hucksters, right?

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:08.250
And there's an obnoxious
amount of hucksters

00:07:08.250 --> 00:07:10.270
in the cannabis industry now.

00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:12.170
A, because they just like smoking weed,

00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:13.290
and they think they're gonna be

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:15.280
fucking the Mark Zuckerberg of weed,

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:18.330
or, two, 'cause they see it as a trend,

00:07:18.330 --> 00:07:21.283
and they wanna cash in fast.

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:23.750
To me, it's very simple.

00:07:23.750 --> 00:07:25.910
It's a simple as this:

00:07:25.910 --> 00:07:29.450
life is actually a marathon.

00:07:29.450 --> 00:07:33.593
Wouldn't be a good idea
to train for a marathon?

00:07:35.390 --> 00:07:36.880
You know, it's that framework.

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:39.140
Like be patient.

00:07:39.140 --> 00:07:41.790
Don't like invest all
your money on something

00:07:41.790 --> 00:07:43.960
that seems like this is the great,

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:48.230
this new formation of cannabis
or hemp is gonna be like,

00:07:48.230 --> 00:07:51.530
people are chasing a lottery ticket

00:07:51.530 --> 00:07:54.030
instead of thinking about what
I want them to think about,

00:07:54.030 --> 00:07:56.350
which is you have a ton of
24 years old in the industry,

00:07:56.350 --> 00:07:57.510
30 year olds,

00:07:57.510 --> 00:08:01.937
and saying, "Okay, I'm gonna
be in this for 20 years.

00:08:01.937 --> 00:08:04.037
"20 years.

00:08:04.037 --> 00:08:05.287
"How am I gonna do that?"

00:08:08.210 --> 00:08:09.760
And so that's what I do, right?

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:13.100
Like, VaynerMedia is now in year nine.

00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:15.800
I've only really run it for seven years.

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:17.580
I've always thought about it long term.

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:20.640
And now it's 900 people.

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:23.610
And now it's hundreds of
millions of dollars in revenue.

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:24.900
And now it's a big thing.

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:27.240
But a lot of people that started companies

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:28.980
in 2009 through 2011 with me,

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:30.980
my homies from Silicon Valley and others,

00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:32.870
even though they raised
millions, and millions,

00:08:32.870 --> 00:08:34.010
and millions of dollars,

00:08:34.010 --> 00:08:35.953
their companies are out of business now.

00:08:38.390 --> 00:08:39.780
Short term thinking.

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:42.870
And this industry is,

00:08:42.870 --> 00:08:45.780
in the same way that the
cannabis industry over-indexes

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:49.180
on really progressive marketing,

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:53.800
it also over-indexes on
unbelievable impatience,

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:56.190
and way too many people
doing it for the lifestyle,

00:08:56.190 --> 00:08:57.433
not for the business.

00:09:01.442 --> 00:09:03.480
- [Andrea] You talk often about the ways

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:05.140
to communicate with the world.

00:09:05.140 --> 00:09:07.170
Like, so, you write,

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:09.510
you've got audio and you've got video.

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:13.380
I don't think, for me personally,

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:15.910
of course this is just my perspective,

00:09:15.910 --> 00:09:19.090
that this industry has
done a very good job

00:09:19.090 --> 00:09:20.373
of any of those things.

00:09:22.444 --> 00:09:24.980
And so what would you like to see?

00:09:24.980 --> 00:09:27.880
I mean, as an investor in this arena,

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:30.370
now that, you know,

00:09:30.370 --> 00:09:32.710
took up ownership in Green Street,

00:09:32.710 --> 00:09:35.230
what would you like to
see this industry do

00:09:35.230 --> 00:09:39.110
as far as, you know,
writing, audio, and video,

00:09:39.110 --> 00:09:41.220
to educate the masses,

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:46.220
and get people to realize
what cannabis is all about?

00:09:47.744 --> 00:09:49.240
Because it's different
things for different people.

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:51.530
- There's two things that aren't
happening to your question,

00:09:51.530 --> 00:09:53.600
one, there's just not
enough people creating,

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:54.640
they're not doing.

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:56.040
They're not fucking working.

00:09:56.950 --> 00:09:58.200
And number two,

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:01.130
they're being too ideological and romantic

00:10:01.130 --> 00:10:03.630
instead of speaking truths.

00:10:03.630 --> 00:10:05.690
I'm one of the most popular entrepreneurs

00:10:05.690 --> 00:10:06.840
in our current society,

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:07.823
no question.

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:09.880
Super proud of that accomplishment.

00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:12.830
I also find it intriguing
that I'm probably the one

00:10:12.830 --> 00:10:17.320
that talks most about the
shortcoming of entrepreneurship,

00:10:17.320 --> 00:10:19.660
and how many fake entrepreneurs there are.

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:21.893
I'm not getting high on my own supply.

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:26.370
- [Andrea] You're not?

00:10:26.370 --> 00:10:27.600
- I'm not.

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:30.430
You know, I think I'm being
critical and thoughtful

00:10:30.430 --> 00:10:31.340
about the game.

00:10:31.340 --> 00:10:32.620
And I think that one of the things

00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:33.850
I see in the cannabis industry,

00:10:33.850 --> 00:10:35.130
is that no matter what conversation

00:10:35.130 --> 00:10:36.373
I have with somebody about it,

00:10:36.373 --> 00:10:38.510
they're one Team Cannabis without seeing

00:10:38.510 --> 00:10:40.123
any other part of it, right?

00:10:41.785 --> 00:10:42.710
It's blind buy in.

00:10:42.710 --> 00:10:45.170
So what I would say is more
work and more articles,

00:10:45.170 --> 00:10:47.730
and audio and video output,

00:10:47.730 --> 00:10:50.510
and more authentic conversations.

00:10:50.510 --> 00:10:52.140
Like what are you actually thinking,

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:53.300
not what's in the best,

00:10:53.300 --> 00:10:55.220
like, do you know how many
people are just saying things

00:10:55.220 --> 00:10:57.240
that are in the best
financial interest of them

00:10:57.240 --> 00:10:58.363
in the short term?

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:00.770
- [Andrea] Too many?

00:11:00.770 --> 00:11:02.270
- Most.

00:11:02.270 --> 00:11:03.103
- [Andrea] Well, yeah,

00:11:03.103 --> 00:11:04.733
I mean, so, it's interesting,

00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:07.670
we're about to start a big project here,

00:11:07.670 --> 00:11:10.470
where we're gonna really
criticize the cannabis community.

00:11:10.470 --> 00:11:12.930
We're gonna do thick
investigative articles,

00:11:12.930 --> 00:11:14.857
where we're gonna dive in deep,

00:11:14.857 --> 00:11:16.490
and we're gonna say, you know what?

00:11:16.490 --> 00:11:18.430
When people legalized cannabis,

00:11:18.430 --> 00:11:19.400
they voted for it.

00:11:19.400 --> 00:11:23.270
They did so under the guys
who promised a lot of things.

00:11:23.270 --> 00:11:25.287
And, so, are we living as a community,

00:11:25.287 --> 00:11:26.700
and as an industry,

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:31.303
and as a sort of new industry,

00:11:31.303 --> 00:11:34.270
like are we living up to the promises

00:11:34.270 --> 00:11:35.523
that we put forth?

00:11:37.254 --> 00:11:40.710
And it will be very
interesting to sort of put

00:11:40.710 --> 00:11:42.340
people's toes in hot water,

00:11:42.340 --> 00:11:44.197
and asking them hard questions.

00:11:46.065 --> 00:11:48.027
Because it's very easy
to sort of sit back,

00:11:48.027 --> 00:11:49.017
and say--

00:11:52.180 --> 00:11:55.320
- I wish you could see the
size of my smile right now.

00:11:55.320 --> 00:11:57.030
I'm so proud of your guys,

00:11:57.030 --> 00:12:00.780
because it's very hard
to be in an industry

00:12:00.780 --> 00:12:02.100
that's this young,

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:05.820
and not just become the press
release for the people in it.

00:12:05.820 --> 00:12:07.680
But what you're actually gonna,

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:09.860
is actually bring value to it.

00:12:09.860 --> 00:12:11.653
I mean, I'm telling you right now,

00:12:12.490 --> 00:12:14.630
my creative curiosity,

00:12:14.630 --> 00:12:16.500
my entrepreneurial curiosity,

00:12:16.500 --> 00:12:19.340
and the fact that I know I
can bring real tangible value

00:12:19.340 --> 00:12:20.560
to this industry,

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:23.110
is the only reason you and
I are talking right now.

00:12:25.930 --> 00:12:28.893
- [Andrea] I mean, I appreciate your time.

00:12:32.166 --> 00:12:34.210
I've listened to a lot of your videos,

00:12:34.210 --> 00:12:36.380
your podcasts that are out there,

00:12:36.380 --> 00:12:37.610
interviews.

00:12:37.610 --> 00:12:41.354
And I think you've definitely
got something to say.

00:12:41.354 --> 00:12:42.323
That's for sure.

00:12:44.623 --> 00:12:48.870
I keep hearing you say
that you don't like being

00:12:48.870 --> 00:12:51.060
sort of pegged as pundit,

00:12:51.060 --> 00:12:52.300
or a guru,

00:12:52.300 --> 00:12:54.353
or a motivational speaker.

00:12:57.043 --> 00:12:58.043
Why is that?

00:12:59.662 --> 00:13:01.357
And why not?

00:13:01.357 --> 00:13:03.230
- That's a great question.

00:13:03.230 --> 00:13:05.930
I don't want that because
it's not the truth.

00:13:05.930 --> 00:13:10.250
I spend 85% of my time being an executive

00:13:10.250 --> 00:13:13.840
of a 900-person, four-office company.

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:16.190
And I think it's important,

00:13:16.190 --> 00:13:18.130
because I only think the truth wins.

00:13:18.130 --> 00:13:20.330
And so that's number one.

00:13:20.330 --> 00:13:22.870
Number two, the reason I don't,

00:13:22.870 --> 00:13:26.160
is 'cause I respect execution too much.

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:28.440
And I think that only being a speaker,

00:13:28.440 --> 00:13:29.560
only being an author,

00:13:29.560 --> 00:13:31.180
only being a pundit,

00:13:31.180 --> 00:13:33.840
is pontificating and not doing.

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:38.530
And I love to pontificate as
my extracurricular activity

00:13:38.530 --> 00:13:40.110
to my actual doing.

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:43.250
I also am even more exciting
that my pontifications

00:13:43.250 --> 00:13:45.283
come from the outputs of my doing.

00:13:47.810 --> 00:13:48.830
Very simply,

00:13:48.830 --> 00:13:51.280
I think it's a lot better to take advice

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:53.120
from somebody who's done
the things they're fucking

00:13:53.120 --> 00:13:54.083
talking about.

00:13:55.690 --> 00:13:56.830
- [Andrea] Well, fuck yeah.

00:13:56.830 --> 00:13:57.663
- Right?

00:13:57.663 --> 00:13:59.240
It's super hard for me to get fired up

00:13:59.240 --> 00:14:01.520
about a 20 year old life coach,

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:03.080
when they're lived 20 years.

00:14:03.080 --> 00:14:04.370
It's super fucking,

00:14:04.370 --> 00:14:06.270
I have a lot of cynicism for somebody

00:14:06.270 --> 00:14:07.560
who writes a business book

00:14:07.560 --> 00:14:11.123
when they've been a professor
at Yale their whole lives.

00:14:14.690 --> 00:14:16.290
- [Andrea] You talk a lot of about empathy

00:14:16.290 --> 00:14:17.673
and sort of compassion.

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:23.570
And not sort of getting
insulted by what other people

00:14:23.570 --> 00:14:24.430
think of you.

00:14:24.430 --> 00:14:26.933
Specifically, you always
say, like your parents.

00:14:33.012 --> 00:14:37.047
What do you say to
someone who is constantly

00:14:39.220 --> 00:14:42.240
very aware of like what
other people think,

00:14:42.240 --> 00:14:45.523
and sort of subscribe to that ideology

00:14:45.523 --> 00:14:47.353
of sort of people-pleasing?

00:14:48.525 --> 00:14:52.460
I mean, how do you get
someone out of that mindset?

00:14:52.460 --> 00:14:54.650
- To me, I think the
only way you get anybody

00:14:54.650 --> 00:14:55.710
out of any mindset,

00:14:55.710 --> 00:14:58.270
is to paint pictures for them to look at

00:14:58.270 --> 00:14:59.500
that are different from the pictures

00:14:59.500 --> 00:15:00.840
that they're looking at.

00:15:00.840 --> 00:15:03.620
And, so, for me, I'm on
this constant mission

00:15:03.620 --> 00:15:04.630
to make people understand,

00:15:04.630 --> 00:15:06.460
the quickest way to be unhappy in the life

00:15:06.460 --> 00:15:08.760
is to let somebody else
dictate your emotions.

00:15:10.930 --> 00:15:12.270
And the truth is,

00:15:12.270 --> 00:15:13.880
the people that dictate your emotions

00:15:13.880 --> 00:15:16.720
tend to be the people
that are closest to you,

00:15:16.720 --> 00:15:18.070
starting with your parents.

00:15:20.230 --> 00:15:22.530
I mean, it's just fucking real.

00:15:22.530 --> 00:15:25.230
I mean, are you kidding me?

00:15:25.230 --> 00:15:26.930
Everybody's issues,

00:15:26.930 --> 00:15:29.113
everybody's issues is with their parents.

00:15:29.990 --> 00:15:31.670
Like all of them.

00:15:31.670 --> 00:15:33.170
Obviously, not every single person.

00:15:33.170 --> 00:15:34.860
But I'm talking, like, I don't know,

00:15:34.860 --> 00:15:35.990
90-something-percent.

00:15:35.990 --> 00:15:37.643
I mean, it's a real thing, you know?

00:15:43.310 --> 00:15:45.539
- [Andrea] At one point,

00:15:45.539 --> 00:15:46.920
when I did my research on you,

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:50.303
you sort of critiqued the creatives.

00:15:53.515 --> 00:15:54.713
But they're necessary.

00:15:55.615 --> 00:15:57.210
And we need people that are creative,

00:15:57.210 --> 00:16:01.106
and then we need sort of the test people,

00:16:01.106 --> 00:16:01.939
so to speak.

00:16:01.939 --> 00:16:03.050
But what advice,

00:16:03.050 --> 00:16:04.830
I mean, we're in a creative industry,

00:16:04.830 --> 00:16:06.090
what we do here at this magazine,

00:16:06.090 --> 00:16:07.650
there isn't one single person here,

00:16:07.650 --> 00:16:09.050
maybe other than our finance person,

00:16:09.050 --> 00:16:11.450
who isn't creative.

00:16:11.450 --> 00:16:16.450
What advice would you
give to creatives in 2018?

00:16:16.750 --> 00:16:18.523
You know, there's so many options.

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:21.510
- Yeah, make more stuff.

00:16:21.510 --> 00:16:22.640
People less subjective.

00:16:22.640 --> 00:16:26.610
Recognize that your ego and power trip

00:16:26.610 --> 00:16:29.210
is the reason that you're
trying to be perfect

00:16:29.210 --> 00:16:31.500
or spending too much time on stuff.

00:16:31.500 --> 00:16:33.710
We live in a world where you
can get instant feedback.

00:16:33.710 --> 00:16:35.050
We can create a lot more content.

00:16:35.050 --> 00:16:36.460
There's a lot more distribution.

00:16:36.460 --> 00:16:38.080
Wrap your head around it.

00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:41.090
You can't be a creative that
lives in the subjectiveness

00:16:41.090 --> 00:16:43.140
of your own point of view.

00:16:43.140 --> 00:16:46.120
Otherwise you'll become
obsolete in the environment

00:16:46.120 --> 00:16:46.953
we now live in.

